=== layout: one-panel

# [Multiplication Rules]{color=orange}

@body

## [AND: multiply along a route]{color=orange}

=== layout: one-panel

# [Special Multiplication Rule]{color=orange}

@body

- The [multiplication rule]{color=orange} states that the probability that two or more [independent]{color=red} events will occur [together]{color=green} is the product of their individual probabilities
  - [P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)]{color=orange}
- Probability in an F1 monohybrid cross can be determined using the [multiplication rule]{color=orange}
- Segregation in a heterozygous plant is like flipping a coin\: Each gamete has a ½ chance of carrying the dominant allele and a ½ chance of carrying the recessive allele

=== layout: one-panel

# [General Multiplication Rule]{color=orange}

@body

- The probability that Events A and B both occur is equal to the probability that Event A occurs times the probability that Event B occurs, given that A has occurred, P(B|A).
  - [P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B|A) = P(B) × P(A|B)]{color=orange}
    - P(A|B) = The probability that Event A occurs, given that Event B has occurred, is called a conditional probability.
- If A and B are [independent]{color=red}, then\:
  - [P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)]{color=orange}

=== layout: two-panels

# Conditional Probability

@left

- The conditional probability of Event B, given Event A has happened, is denoted by the symbol P(B|A).
  - If A and B are independent then P(B|A) = P(B)
- Conversion\:
  - P(B|A) = P(A and B)/P(A)
  - P(A|B) = P(A and B)/P(B)

@right

