@top-left

![Slide image 9](assets/lect05d-probability_concepts/image_009.png)

@top-right

- Part (A)
  - Because it is rare, assume father heterozygous
  - 50% chance young man received bad allele

@bottom

- A: young man has HD allele; P(A) = 1/2
- B: child receives HD allele; P(B|A) = 1/2
- [P(A and B) = P(B|A) x P(A) = 1/4]{color=orange}

=== layout: two-panels


@left

![Slide image 9](assets/lect05d-probability_concepts/image_009.png)

@right

- Part (A)
  - Because it is rare, assume father heterozygous
  - 50% chance young man received bad allele

- P(B|A) = child has HD given that the young man has HD allele = 50%
- P(A|B) = young man has HD given that his son has HD allele = 50%

- Part (B)
  - A\: chance that young man had bad allele
  - B\: chance that child will get bad allele
  - [P(A and B) = P(B|A) × P(A)]{color=orange}
  - P(B|A) = 50%
  - P(A) = 50%
  - [P(A and B) = 25%]{color=orange}
  - [P(A and B) = P(A|B) × P(B)]{color=orange}
  - 25% = 100% × P(B); P(B) = 100%

=== layout: section

# Summary

=== layout: one-panel

# [Multiplication Rules]{color=orange}

@body

- [Specific Multiplication Rule]{color=orange}, if two events, A and B, are [independent]{color=red}, then\:
  - [P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)]{color=orange}
- [General Multiplication Rule]{color=orange}, if two events, A and B, are NOT independent then\:
  - [P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B|A) = P(B) × P(A|B)]{color=orange}

=== layout: one-panel

# [Addition Rules]{color=blue}

@body

- [Specific Addition Rule]{color=blue}\: if two events, A and B, are [mutually exclusive]{color=red}, then\:
  - [P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)]{color=blue}
- [General Addition Rule]{color=blue}\: if two events, A and B, are NOT mutually exclusive, then\:
  - [P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) &ndash; P(A and B)]{color=blue}

=== layout: one-panel

# Summary: check the rule's condition

@body

- [OR: add probabilities; subtract any overlap]{color=blue}
- [AND: multiply; account for dependence]{color=orange}
- Use frequencies among successful gametes
- Read "given" as a restriction on possible outcomes

@notes

Connect each rule to its condition. Return to the opening pollen cross and distinguish
the gametes produced from those that contribute to offspring.

